WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.360
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:25.810
Hi there.

9
00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:27.850
Today we're going to talk about Angry Andy.

10
00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:29.210
He's very angry.

11
00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:31.330
He's very angry.

12
00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:33.750
This is Season 8, Episode 21.

13
00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:37.870
It was written by Justin Spitzer and directed by Claire Scanlon.

14
00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:39.190
Would you like a summary?

15
00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:40.490
Would I ever.

16
00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:47.350
Well, now that he's back in Scranton, Andy struggles to regain his job, his office, and his manhood.

17
00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:51.390
Because Nellie stole his office, stole the manager position.

18
00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:54.910
And I guess maybe his ability to perform in other places.

19
00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:58.690
Yeah, she's really stolen all of his performance opportunities.

20
00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:06.550
Meanwhile, Ryan tries to win Kelly back after seeing her move on with Pam and Jim's handsome pediatrician, Ravi.

21
00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:09.490
Who is very handsome, by the way.

22
00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:10.090
I mean, dapper.

23
00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:13.390
Well, everyone, we had a new director for this episode.

24
00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:18.130
It was Claire Scanlon, who joined The Office family in Season 5 as an editor.

25
00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:22.130
She directed two episodes of the show, including this one.

26
00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:25.110
Angry Andy was her directorial debut.

27
00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.970
And she now directs all the time.

28
00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:30.830
She has been teamed up with lots of Office aluMs.

29
00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:40.830
She's directed episodes of Unbreakable Kimmy Schmidt, Never Have I Ever, Abbott Elementary, as well as Brooklyn Nine-Nine, Black-ish, and Fresh Off the Boat, Angela,

30
00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:42.430
our new crossover connection.

31
00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:44.230
And guess what, everyone?

32
00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:49.870
I'm telling you all of this, not just because we love Claire, but because she's sitting here right now with us!

33
00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:50.390
Yay!

34
00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:52.990
My God, such enthusiasm.

35
00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:57.010
Can you wake up with me every morning and get me ready for the day?

36
00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:57.250
Yeah.

37
00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:01.950
I'll just send Jenna over with, like, your life summary as you pour coffee.

38
00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:02.250
I love this.

39
00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:04.090
We're so excited you're here.

40
00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:05.990
I'm really, really honored to be here.

41
00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:08.510
My goodness, what you guys have created is so phenomenal.

42
00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:11.470
Everyone's always talking to me about this podcaSt.

43
00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:11.790
Really?

44
00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:12.410
And you guys always.

45
00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:12.870
They are?

46
00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:13.790
Absolutely.

47
00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:17.350
When I'm on a set, people I work with, like, everyone in comedy.

48
00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.130
I mean, and then, of course, fans are obsessed with it.

49
00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:21.450
So, as you already know.

50
00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:22.050
But, yeah.

51
00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:26.490
No, and you guys were, like, on the cutting edge of doing podcasts.

52
00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:27.190
Like, it's huge.

53
00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:28.090
Well, you come to my house.

54
00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:28.490
I know!

55
00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:29.710
That's what I was going to say.

56
00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:31.070
Claire, come wake me up.

57
00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:35.550
Okay, Claire, we have lots of questions for you.

58
00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:37.390
I know fans are going to be so excited.

59
00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:39.570
You have such a unique perspective on the show.

60
00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:44.330
So, I was cracking up because before you came here today, you sent us some pictures.

61
00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:45.910
You sent a picture.

62
00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:47.990
Claire and I have known each other a very long time.

63
00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:49.430
Claire, how long have we known each other?

64
00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:51.490
I mean, older than some people listening.

65
00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:57.950
I would say at least 20 years, if not 25.

66
00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:01.450
I think it was our mid-20s when you were doing Groundlings.

67
00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:02.310
Yes.

68
00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:04.070
Oh, well, that was 20.

69
00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:06.110
Yeah, that was a long time ago.

70
00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:10.910
So, Claire sent a picture, and I have this picture somewhere, too, at a little slightly

71
00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:13.430
different angle because I think a few people were taking it.

72
00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:15.930
I'm in full Western wear.

73
00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:18.990
I have a Western shirt, a cowboy hat.

74
00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:22.310
We are sitting at a bar, and we are doing tequila shots.

75
00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:23.250
Yeah, that's accurate.

76
00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:24.790
Was it my birthday?

77
00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:25.890
No.

78
00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:28.370
I can't remember why we were doing that.

79
00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:29.970
Why were you dressed that way?

80
00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:35.670
Also, was that how you dressed back in the middle 25 years ago?

81
00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:39.510
I do remember one year from my birthday, I wore, like, my dad's cowboy hat.

82
00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:41.070
I guess I really was fond of it.

83
00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:42.010
I went through a phase.

84
00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:44.430
It was the 90s.

85
00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.350
Yeah, I mean, I feel like that phase works for Taylor Swift today, so.

86
00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:48.190
Thank you.

87
00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:49.770
Okay, Swifties.

88
00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:51.430
You're on the cutting edge of everything.

89
00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:57.850
I do remember, Claire, though, of all of our friends, you were one of us that had their

90
00:03:57.850 --> 00:03:58.370
shit together.

91
00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:02.290
You had, like, really good jobs, and you were very responsible.

92
00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:08.730
You weren't showing up doing tequila shots in a cowboy hat, and I remember you were working

93
00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:10.430
on a lot of documentaries back then.

94
00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:11.090
You were editing.

95
00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:15.930
I remember going to a screening of one and being like, oh, my gosh, she's, like, the real

96
00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:16.270
deal.

97
00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:22.530
So we always ask our guests, how did they get their job on their office, and can you tell

98
00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:26.030
us how you ended up working on the show and how you came to direct this episode?

99
00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:26.730
Absolutely.

100
00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:31.710
I went to grad school at USC, and my very last class was editing.

101
00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:36.370
I learned Avid, and my TA said, I don't want to be an assistant editor.

102
00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:37.490
Does anyone want my job?

103
00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:39.190
I want to go into cinematography.

104
00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:43.510
And I said, I want a job out of grad school to start paying my student loans.

105
00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:46.210
Of course, because this is who I remember you were.

106
00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:48.610
Yeah, and I was like, I will take that job.

107
00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:53.030
And I worked with this wonderful editor named Arnold Glassman on a project called Frank

108
00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:54.170
Kapper's American Dream.

109
00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:54.670
Yes.

110
00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:58.970
That was really my first job ever in this industry.

111
00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:01.990
And it was the first high-def documentary ever.

112
00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:04.070
Like, high-definition television didn't exiSt.

113
00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:05.230
This was, like, 1997.

114
00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:07.950
So this was one of their first projects ever.

115
00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:14.330
But I also worked on a lot of heavy news subject material, like Dying to Tell the Story, which

116
00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:19.270
is about Dan Eldon, who was stoned to death in Somalia, Soldiers of Peace, about these kids

117
00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:25.150
who were nominated for a Nobel Peace Prize for trying to get people in Colombia to vote.

118
00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:28.850
And then I just kind of veered onto more, not intentionally.

119
00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:30.590
I just liked working.

120
00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:32.230
I liked working with Arnold Glassman.

121
00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:33.910
And I also just liked editing.

122
00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:36.050
I worked a lot with a woman named Kira Thompson.

123
00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:37.150
She was amazing.

124
00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:43.950
And it led to me doing a couple of documentaries about celebrities that were known for comedy.

125
00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:48.470
Carol Burnett, A Woman of Character, Bob Newhart, Unbuttoned, Cary Grant, A Class Apart.

126
00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:50.510
These were all American masters.

127
00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:54.370
And I really enjoyed watching the canon of their work.

128
00:05:54.550 --> 00:06:00.650
So without even realizing what I was doing, I was getting such an education, both in comedy

129
00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:01.850
and comedy editing.

130
00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:04.670
You know, and again, not meaning to.

131
00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:06.550
It wasn't, like, calculated in any way.

132
00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:11.130
And then I was working on Last Comic Standing, and I was a supervising producer and supervising

133
00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.970
editor of that particular season.

134
00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:20.470
And I get a phone call from my friend Grady Cooper, and he said, hey, I hear that there's

135
00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:22.090
this new spinoff of The Office.

136
00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:23.230
Can you put my name in the hat?

137
00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:24.910
Because I know you're friendly with Paul Lieberstein.

138
00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:30.690
And the same way I know Angela, I know Warren, I know Paul, we all just, like, hung around in

139
00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:32.730
our 20s, just whenever someone wasn't working.

140
00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:33.590
I was like, want to play tennis?

141
00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:35.710
Like, it was just really casual in our 20s.

142
00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:37.130
So I said, oh, yeah, sure.

143
00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:37.790
I'd be happy to.

144
00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:41.390
And so I called Paul, and I said, my friend wants to put his name in the hat to edit.

145
00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:42.830
And he said, well, what about you?

146
00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:46.270
And I said, oh, that's so great.

147
00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:50.370
And there were 30 people up for the replacement for Dean Holland.

148
00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:54.850
Dean Holland went on to become the supervising producer and editor of Parks and Rec.

149
00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:58.290
And he left a hole that I was very happy to fill.

150
00:06:58.510 --> 00:07:00.170
I still remember my job interview.

151
00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:05.230
It was with Greg, Mike Schur, Paul Lieberstein, and Jen Salata.

152
00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:06.290
They were all in there.

153
00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:09.910
And Greg was just, like, rapid-fire questions.

154
00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:14.770
Luckily, I don't think I was nearly as intimidated as I should have been.

155
00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:19.370
And I just didn't have anything to lose, because already I was never even considering this,

156
00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:20.930
because I'd never edited narrative.

157
00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:22.430
I'd never, ever.

158
00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:24.810
I'd only done documentary and sketch comedy.

159
00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:27.810
And this was not in my wheelhouse.

160
00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:29.550
Which Greg probably loved, though.

161
00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:30.330
He loved that.

162
00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:33.690
He loved that I came with no baggage, he said.

163
00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:34.750
So I got the job.

164
00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:38.010
There was a little bit of, like, a let's see if it goes all right.

165
00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:38.790
And I guess it did.

166
00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:44.130
Because at the end of season five, three of the three editors on The Office were nominated for Emmys that year.

167
00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:45.390
So everyone did well.

168
00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:52.470
And then Dave Rogers and I continued and edited all the way through the end of the—so I was there for about five years.

169
00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:54.670
Started on season five and went through nine.

170
00:07:54.970 --> 00:08:02.030
Well, I just remember how seamlessly you fell into the fabric of The Office family.

171
00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:06.790
Like, just your personality, your take on things.

172
00:08:06.790 --> 00:08:08.410
It was an immediate fit.

173
00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:18.090
Like, whatever you brought to that interview, it was clear probably to them and then immediately clear to the rest of us that you were like, oh, yeah, of course it's her.

174
00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:20.010
It was wonderful to be there.

175
00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:21.930
I mean, it was a very atypical situation.

176
00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:24.090
I mean, I'm sure there's other situations like that.

177
00:08:24.190 --> 00:08:34.030
But to be all under one roof, to sit and eat lunch together, to have such pleasant working hours, cross-coverage that led to so many great comedic moments,

178
00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:35.450
the electricity on the set.

179
00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:39.350
Like, I remember whenever something cool was happening, we would just be like, they're doing something cool.

180
00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:39.750
Let's go.

181
00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:40.050
Run.

182
00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:43.490
And we'd run across the parking lot and go see something freaking cool.

183
00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:45.330
Like, there's a porcupine in a drawer.

184
00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:45.750
Run.

185
00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:47.190
Rain's got a dream.

186
00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:50.690
Like, so everyone just wanted to be part of the cool, fun thing.

187
00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:51.270
Totally.

188
00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:52.370
I just want to watch it.

189
00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:53.930
That was the spirit of the show.

190
00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:54.650
It really was.

191
00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:55.510
It was like we were kids at school.

192
00:08:55.510 --> 00:08:55.890
Let's be a part of it.

193
00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:57.990
Someone was doing something cool in the art room.

194
00:08:58.290 --> 00:08:58.490
Yeah.

195
00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:02.710
You know, it was really special, and I've been chasing that ever since, you know.

196
00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:03.330
Same.

197
00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:04.710
Yeah, same.

198
00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:07.770
How did you come to direct this episode?

199
00:09:07.970 --> 00:09:12.550
Had you said, you know what, I'd be really interested in directing an episode, or did someone come to you?

200
00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:17.450
I think that both Dean Holland and Dave Rogers led the way for editors directing on this show,

201
00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:25.950
and I think it was a combination of those two gentlemen having paved the way and Greg's esteem for editors.

202
00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:28.130
Like, he really is special.

203
00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:32.350
And I think that also has showrunners really do appreciate the editors.

204
00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:41.170
Like, a strong editor is someone you really are happy to have because it ultimately means less work for the showrunner and less hours in a dark room.

205
00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:49.830
So because they had done that, it was season eight, and I think there were some open slots, and I thought I'd just give it a go.

206
00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:51.950
And then I went to Paul and Greg.

207
00:09:51.950 --> 00:09:57.850
Okay, so now you mentioned that you edited your own episode.

208
00:09:58.210 --> 00:09:59.810
I was a little surprised.

209
00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:03.130
I wondered, like, if you would prefer it.

210
00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:05.750
Do you prefer editing your directing?

211
00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:11.990
So it's a very specific question for editing Angry Andy and whether I wanted to edit that versus today.

212
00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:12.550
Okay.

213
00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:17.550
So I absolutely wanted to edit Angry Andy in case I messed up.

214
00:10:17.930 --> 00:10:19.130
No one would know.

215
00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:20.410
I could fix it.

216
00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:21.850
You could cover it in editing.

217
00:10:22.030 --> 00:10:22.850
No one would know.

218
00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:24.670
Like, how did she not get that other edit?

219
00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:26.710
I'm going to just razzle-dazz you with something.

220
00:10:27.250 --> 00:10:29.370
And you'll never know I didn't get that coverage.

221
00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:33.310
I didn't get that shot that any more season director might have gotten.

222
00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:37.750
So I was very, very—I think I must have come in over the weekend so no one could even see me working on it.

223
00:10:38.030 --> 00:10:40.450
Today, I really don't think it's a good idea.

224
00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:44.250
I know Steven Soderbergh does it, so hats off to him and the Coen brothers.

225
00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:49.350
I think, especially in comedy, you can get a bit myopic and have set love.

226
00:10:49.530 --> 00:10:51.690
And I'm sure every actor feels this way, too.

227
00:10:51.870 --> 00:10:52.990
Something was hilarious.

228
00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:53.890
You know it hit.

229
00:10:53.990 --> 00:10:54.910
The crew was laughing.

230
00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:56.570
It took forever to set up the shot.

231
00:10:56.570 --> 00:10:57.450
It all worked beautifully.

232
00:10:57.450 --> 00:11:04.110
And you just felt this kind of jubilation that isn't there when you watch it in the Avid on a television monitor.

233
00:11:04.150 --> 00:11:04.810
It's just not there.

234
00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:07.890
And you have an editor be like, yeah, I don't care how long it took you to set up this shot.

235
00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:08.370
It's not good.

236
00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:09.090
It's not funny.

237
00:11:09.250 --> 00:11:10.290
I didn't laugh, and I don't care.

238
00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:12.210
And you don't need to push the story forward.

239
00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:18.090
And I like having that person that doesn't have, you know, attachment to it the way you might.

240
00:11:18.290 --> 00:11:20.290
The whole industry is a very collaborative one.

241
00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:27.530
And I think that what was great about The Office is that everyone was firing on all cylinders and listening to everyone else.

242
00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:33.770
I think that's the key is listening because you can have a lot of creative people, but if they don't listen to one another, it's going to be a train wreck.

243
00:11:34.190 --> 00:11:34.550
Absolutely.

244
00:11:34.930 --> 00:11:36.550
And not only were they listening, they were encouraging.

245
00:11:36.910 --> 00:11:38.650
Like, everyone was a friendly ear.

246
00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:39.050
Yeah.

247
00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:41.330
And it wasn't scary in any way, shape, or form.

248
00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:43.030
And that's the set I walked onto.

249
00:11:43.030 --> 00:11:46.810
And, you know, Matt Sohn came in the weekend before to help me prep.

250
00:11:47.030 --> 00:11:49.730
That's the director of photography who also directed himself.

251
00:11:50.570 --> 00:11:53.530
Jen Salata gave me this wonderful document.

252
00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:58.170
Okay, you shared this document with us, and our listeners know Jen Salata.

253
00:11:58.310 --> 00:11:59.210
She's been on the show.

254
00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:00.870
We love her dearly.

255
00:12:01.130 --> 00:12:04.330
Will you share some of the advice that she gave you?

256
00:12:04.450 --> 00:12:05.450
I have to open up my computer.

257
00:12:05.710 --> 00:12:06.790
You still have it.

258
00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:09.370
You said you have it saved on your desktop.

259
00:12:09.370 --> 00:12:09.730
It is.

260
00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:11.710
I mean, you can actually see it's on my desktop.

