491
00:21:27.890 --> 00:21:28.130
Right.

492
00:21:28.250 --> 00:21:28.970
It was huge.

493
00:21:29.310 --> 00:21:30.030
It's a huge thing.

494
00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:32.050
And horses are just, also, they have such empathy.

495
00:21:32.330 --> 00:21:32.510
Yes.

496
00:21:32.510 --> 00:21:34.950
That's why they do all of that great therapy work with horses.

497
00:21:34.950 --> 00:21:36.330
That's what my dad does with horses.

498
00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:37.230
That's amazing.

499
00:21:37.250 --> 00:21:39.750
The horses do therapy work with autistic children.

500
00:21:40.010 --> 00:21:40.470
That's awesome.

501
00:21:40.590 --> 00:21:41.550
And he's one of the horse handlers.

502
00:21:41.690 --> 00:21:42.170
Yeah.

503
00:21:42.410 --> 00:21:43.530
That's an amazing job.

504
00:21:43.830 --> 00:21:44.610
That's amazing.

505
00:21:44.810 --> 00:21:47.350
How gratifying, because I'm sure he sees the difference.

506
00:21:47.570 --> 00:21:48.390
It's like night and day.

507
00:21:48.470 --> 00:21:48.610
Yeah.

508
00:21:48.810 --> 00:21:50.150
And horses are so smart.

509
00:21:50.310 --> 00:21:50.850
So smart.

510
00:21:50.850 --> 00:21:51.470
So smart.

511
00:21:51.470 --> 00:21:57.710
I noticed in the script that the throwing of the caramels was not scripted originally.

512
00:21:58.510 --> 00:22:01.810
Erin was just supposed to throw the pens and throw the messages.

513
00:22:02.270 --> 00:22:04.670
And then she throws those caramels.

514
00:22:04.790 --> 00:22:09.490
And I felt like, when I was watching, the reason I went to the script was because I felt

515
00:22:09.490 --> 00:22:14.750
like Catherine Tate was legitimately surprised that she was having to dodge caramels.

516
00:22:15.830 --> 00:22:16.390
Yeah.

517
00:22:16.390 --> 00:22:19.310
I feel like everyone kept Catherine on her toes on that show.

518
00:22:20.050 --> 00:22:20.610
Yeah.

519
00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:20.790
Yeah.

520
00:22:21.830 --> 00:22:25.270
Well, was there anything else you prepped special for Angry Andy?

521
00:22:25.590 --> 00:22:30.330
When preparing to direct Angry Andy, it was the first time that we would be seeing Nellie

522
00:22:30.330 --> 00:22:32.090
as manager in that office space.

523
00:22:32.230 --> 00:22:35.730
And that office space for the manager is so iconic.

524
00:22:36.090 --> 00:22:36.550
Yes.

525
00:22:36.970 --> 00:22:38.530
And how it's decorated.

526
00:22:38.650 --> 00:22:38.990
Exactly.

527
00:22:39.050 --> 00:22:39.990
With her so fun.

528
00:22:40.390 --> 00:22:41.170
And her throw.

529
00:22:41.230 --> 00:22:47.710
And I wanted to make sure that we had enough time to establish the space, but also keep things

530
00:22:47.710 --> 00:22:48.810
interesting with Andy.

531
00:22:48.810 --> 00:22:54.470
So a way in which to put the things in her office that set deck had deemed worthy of her.

532
00:22:54.570 --> 00:22:56.290
And we also went through that as well.

533
00:22:56.290 --> 00:23:00.470
But also making sure they were placed in such a way that it could organically be revealed

534
00:23:00.470 --> 00:23:02.990
with Andy and Nellie having their standoff.

535
00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:03.350
Right.

536
00:23:03.470 --> 00:23:04.510
Her saying, I'm not leaving.

537
00:23:04.810 --> 00:23:09.570
And then that's where she says, Angela, doc, $100 from Andy's back.

538
00:23:09.570 --> 00:23:16.590
Like crickets when Andy does the same, which is a wonderful payback for Angela to do to

539
00:23:16.590 --> 00:23:16.830
Andy.

540
00:23:17.210 --> 00:23:21.770
And so definitely wanting to make sure that you really felt her presence and how thoroughly

541
00:23:21.770 --> 00:23:24.470
ensconced she was into that new manager's office.

542
00:23:24.910 --> 00:23:26.210
And those scenes were tight.

543
00:23:26.410 --> 00:23:28.970
And you wanted it to feel like a high noon moment.

544
00:23:29.130 --> 00:23:30.650
Like they're against each other.

545
00:23:30.830 --> 00:23:30.990
Yeah.

546
00:23:30.990 --> 00:23:35.450
And just making sure that in that small space, you still got that kind of awkward vibe of

547
00:23:35.450 --> 00:23:37.210
them facing off against each other.

548
00:23:37.570 --> 00:23:40.270
I thought the office was decorated so well.

549
00:23:40.270 --> 00:23:46.190
And it was very clear immediately that all of Andy's things had been wiped from the space

550
00:23:46.190 --> 00:23:48.530
and that it was a totally new space.

551
00:23:48.850 --> 00:23:50.250
So I thought that was great.

552
00:23:50.410 --> 00:23:50.770
Oh, cool.

553
00:23:50.890 --> 00:23:51.550
I'm glad it worked.

554
00:23:51.750 --> 00:23:51.950
Yeah.

555
00:23:52.190 --> 00:23:52.370
It totally worked.

556
00:23:52.370 --> 00:23:55.510
And actually, in re-watching it, I was like, oh, the office is different.

557
00:23:55.650 --> 00:23:56.750
And I was like, yeah, I see it.

558
00:23:56.750 --> 00:23:57.870
You notice it right away.

559
00:23:57.990 --> 00:23:58.190
Yeah.

560
00:23:58.330 --> 00:23:58.410
Yeah.

561
00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:02.870
We always ask everyone, when the show ended, did you take anything?

562
00:24:03.130 --> 00:24:04.490
Because we took lots of stuff.

563
00:24:04.630 --> 00:24:04.790
Okay.

564
00:24:04.990 --> 00:24:05.690
Give it up, Claire.

565
00:24:05.950 --> 00:24:08.270
I got permission from Greg to take.

566
00:24:08.370 --> 00:24:12.470
And I feel now I need to pass it along because I feel like it's such a good thing.

567
00:24:12.510 --> 00:24:12.910
What?

568
00:24:13.110 --> 00:24:13.990
What did you get?

569
00:24:13.990 --> 00:24:16.670
I got the ducky that would ruin takes.

570
00:24:17.750 --> 00:24:18.150
Yes!

571
00:24:18.350 --> 00:24:19.090
I got the ducky.

572
00:24:19.250 --> 00:24:19.490
Oh, my God!

573
00:24:19.630 --> 00:24:21.110
We haven't talked about this stuff.

574
00:24:21.110 --> 00:24:21.650
I have the ducky.

575
00:24:21.650 --> 00:24:22.370
I still have it.

576
00:24:22.450 --> 00:24:23.150
It's on my bookcase.

577
00:24:23.310 --> 00:24:24.730
I think you do deserve that.

578
00:24:24.930 --> 00:24:27.650
Either you or someone from sound deserves that duck.

579
00:24:27.650 --> 00:24:30.770
I felt like everyone's so many takes that I was like—

580
00:24:30.770 --> 00:24:32.510
Made it hard for you to edit things.

581
00:24:32.710 --> 00:24:37.550
But Sarah Levy was in the office when John burst in looking for something.

582
00:24:37.730 --> 00:24:38.870
He hadn't thought it through.

583
00:24:39.070 --> 00:24:40.010
Like, this is over.

584
00:24:40.370 --> 00:24:41.530
And he was like, what can I take when—

585
00:24:41.530 --> 00:24:43.050
And he took the sign off the wall.

586
00:24:43.210 --> 00:24:45.030
He started to go for the ducky.

587
00:24:45.190 --> 00:24:47.350
And Sarah was like, Greg already promised that to Claire.

588
00:24:48.150 --> 00:24:51.250
So now I feel a little bit like,

589
00:24:51.250 --> 00:24:53.110
I've had my time with the ducky.

590
00:24:53.290 --> 00:24:55.130
My kids have actually played with the ducky.

591
00:24:55.270 --> 00:24:55.970
It still works.

592
00:24:56.290 --> 00:24:56.710
Oh, it does.

593
00:24:56.850 --> 00:24:57.410
It still works.

594
00:24:57.590 --> 00:24:58.190
It's so loud.

595
00:24:58.470 --> 00:25:00.150
Isabel would go hit it every time she was on set.

596
00:25:00.150 --> 00:25:00.910
Because it's fun.

597
00:25:01.050 --> 00:25:01.690
It's cute.

598
00:25:01.930 --> 00:25:03.510
So many good bloopers with that ducky.

599
00:25:04.530 --> 00:25:07.650
Well, one of the questions we always end an interview with is,

600
00:25:07.770 --> 00:25:10.790
was there any favorite memory from your time on the show?

601
00:25:11.010 --> 00:25:12.330
Anything you would want to share with us?

602
00:25:13.030 --> 00:25:14.870
I would say there's too many.

603
00:25:15.050 --> 00:25:17.230
And I'm sure that so many people say the same answer.

604
00:25:17.350 --> 00:25:19.170
Especially when you're there day in and day out.

605
00:25:19.310 --> 00:25:21.670
You know, working there for such a long extended period of time.

606
00:25:22.010 --> 00:25:23.650
There's just too many wonderful moments.

607
00:25:23.890 --> 00:25:24.890
It's an impossible question.

608
00:25:24.890 --> 00:25:32.150
But my personal relationship to a scene and a moment would have to be Niagara.

609
00:25:32.830 --> 00:25:39.470
And the reason why it had such fun and comedy in the walking down the aisle to the song.

610
00:25:39.650 --> 00:25:43.070
That was such a moment in the YouTube history of people doing that.

611
00:25:43.230 --> 00:25:44.390
And the comedy there.

612
00:25:44.390 --> 00:25:51.390
But intercutting it with the footage that Randall shot at Niagara Falls with Pam and Jim

613
00:25:51.390 --> 00:25:55.150
when they actually got married versus the one where everyone else thought they got married.

614
00:25:55.590 --> 00:26:02.430
Intercutting that and finding that last shot where Jim is holding Pam

615
00:26:02.430 --> 00:26:05.510
and they're looking out over the water and then he turns to the camera.

616
00:26:06.050 --> 00:26:09.370
That shot, it was like one of those, he knew to do it.

617
00:26:09.510 --> 00:26:10.470
You probably felt it.

618
00:26:10.670 --> 00:26:12.710
It was such a magical shot.

619
00:26:12.710 --> 00:26:16.330
And then getting to show you and John in my Eta Bay.

620
00:26:16.550 --> 00:26:18.810
You guys came in just like we were talking earlier.

621
00:26:19.070 --> 00:26:22.910
Actors would just come in and be like, and you both wanted to see that scene cut.

622
00:26:23.110 --> 00:26:24.390
You know, it was your wedding scene.

623
00:26:24.510 --> 00:26:25.710
You wanted to see it and like show us.

624
00:26:25.970 --> 00:26:29.050
And being so nervous for you to see how I did it.

625
00:26:29.130 --> 00:26:31.530
And then turning around and seeing you guys were both crying.

626
00:26:31.830 --> 00:26:32.730
I remember that.

627
00:26:32.870 --> 00:26:33.010
It was great.

628
00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:38.690
And I was like, and I knew it was that last, it was that last moment where he turns to the camera

629
00:26:38.690 --> 00:26:40.110
and he's like, I did it.

630
00:26:40.270 --> 00:26:41.590
Like in his face.

631
00:26:41.590 --> 00:26:43.230
Oh my God, it's such a great moment.

632
00:26:43.430 --> 00:26:44.210
It's perfect.

633
00:26:44.550 --> 00:26:45.790
It was a perfect moment.

634
00:26:46.190 --> 00:26:52.010
I mean, the appreciation Jenna and I have now in a, I think in a deeper way for all the

635
00:26:52.010 --> 00:26:54.090
departments of the show that made it come together.

636
00:26:54.710 --> 00:27:01.550
But just really seeing how editors are such beautiful storytellers and you're such a beautiful

637
00:27:01.550 --> 00:27:02.250
storyteller.

638
00:27:02.430 --> 00:27:03.250
I'm like teary.

639
00:27:03.410 --> 00:27:04.050
Why am I teary?

640
00:27:04.310 --> 00:27:04.890
I don't know.

641
00:27:04.890 --> 00:27:08.890
I'm just so appreciative that we all got to work together and that we're still in touch

642
00:27:08.890 --> 00:27:09.930
and we get to talk about it.

643
00:27:09.930 --> 00:27:11.350
I get this way with every interview.

644
00:27:11.850 --> 00:27:15.930
I think all of us went through a lot of stuff in our personal lives during this show.

645
00:27:16.310 --> 00:27:19.990
And I can't think of somewhere else I'd rather be during that time.

646
00:27:20.290 --> 00:27:21.770
Like going through everything.

647
00:27:21.970 --> 00:27:24.410
I mean, I can't tell you how supportive you guys were.

648
00:27:24.690 --> 00:27:28.390
Like you two specifically telling me your own personal stories.

649
00:27:28.390 --> 00:27:32.930
She's not even just talking about work, just like in life to go through.

650
00:27:33.350 --> 00:27:35.110
Everyone's going to face obstacles in life.

651
00:27:35.290 --> 00:27:40.010
And to have everyone at your side, at your work, rooting for you and not having to worry

652
00:27:40.010 --> 00:27:43.130
about that part of it was just delightful and special.

653
00:27:43.690 --> 00:27:43.790
Yeah.

654
00:27:44.470 --> 00:27:46.670
Aw, Claire, thank you so much for coming.

655
00:27:46.910 --> 00:27:48.150
This was such a pleasure.

656
00:27:48.530 --> 00:27:49.350
This is so much fun.

657
00:27:49.810 --> 00:27:52.050
Congratulations on all your insane success.

658
00:27:52.970 --> 00:27:53.930
And you too.

659
00:27:54.310 --> 00:27:54.730
I know.

660
00:27:54.870 --> 00:27:55.590
My gosh.

661
00:27:55.790 --> 00:27:57.270
Claire's a big old badass.

662
00:27:58.130 --> 00:27:59.970
Well, we're going to hit you up for questions.

663
00:28:00.430 --> 00:28:00.830
Yeah.

664
00:28:01.010 --> 00:28:01.890
In upcoming episodes.

665
00:28:01.930 --> 00:28:02.570
Sure, sure, sure.

666
00:28:02.770 --> 00:28:02.870
Yeah.

667
00:28:02.970 --> 00:28:03.150
Okay.

668
00:28:03.630 --> 00:28:06.170
Claire, this was an absolute joy seeing you again.

669
00:28:06.370 --> 00:28:07.510
My heart is so full.

670
00:28:07.730 --> 00:28:09.170
I just hung on your every word.

671
00:28:09.310 --> 00:28:11.850
Thank you so much for taking the time to be here with us today.

672
00:28:12.390 --> 00:28:12.730
Yeah.

673
00:28:12.730 --> 00:28:13.610
It was amazing.

674
00:28:13.850 --> 00:28:17.070
I mean, we chatted for a long time before we even started this interview.

675
00:28:17.210 --> 00:28:18.590
And I loved all of it.

676
00:28:18.590 --> 00:28:20.470
But I'm so grateful to both of you.

677
00:28:20.650 --> 00:28:25.390
And I feel like I can't leave without saying how welcome you made someone who was like a

678
00:28:25.390 --> 00:28:30.710
transfer student at the office because I did come in so deep into the series.

679
00:28:30.970 --> 00:28:32.810
You know, I came in halfway through season five.

680
00:28:32.810 --> 00:28:39.150
So I never felt like I was, you know, less than or just that, you know, someone writing

681
00:28:39.150 --> 00:28:40.170
everyone else coattails.

682
00:28:40.370 --> 00:28:43.230
I always felt so included and in every way, shape, or form.

683
00:28:43.470 --> 00:28:48.430
You had an evening where you two treated all the women on the crew.

684
00:28:48.850 --> 00:28:49.850
Out for drinks.

685
00:28:50.070 --> 00:28:50.170
Yeah.

686
00:28:50.230 --> 00:28:50.790
Do you remember that?

687
00:28:50.850 --> 00:28:51.190
I do.

688
00:28:51.310 --> 00:28:51.590
I do.

689
00:28:51.770 --> 00:28:51.910
Yeah.

690
00:28:52.130 --> 00:28:53.810
That's never happened before since.

691
00:28:54.090 --> 00:28:55.250
Like, that's so special.

692
00:28:55.430 --> 00:28:56.030
It was so fun.

693
00:28:56.030 --> 00:28:57.170
It was so fun.

694
00:28:57.310 --> 00:28:59.550
Like, I'm so glad I wanted to mention that.

695
00:28:59.670 --> 00:29:01.170
You know, that's so unique and special.

696
00:29:01.370 --> 00:29:03.090
Like, that just doesn't happen.

697
00:29:03.190 --> 00:29:05.370
And everyone was so grateful for that.

698
00:29:05.490 --> 00:29:07.990
And it just, to me, it's not just that you did that gesture.

699
00:29:07.990 --> 00:29:14.050
It was emblematic of the tone that was set from day one and how it trickled on, all on

700
00:29:14.050 --> 00:29:17.730
down to just such a good Bon Amien atmosphere on the entire show.

701
00:29:17.890 --> 00:29:18.630
So thank you.

702
00:29:18.990 --> 00:29:19.290
Aw.

703
00:29:20.030 --> 00:29:20.810
Thanks, Claire.

704
00:29:20.950 --> 00:29:21.350
Thanks, Claire.

705
00:29:21.470 --> 00:29:21.750
Thank you.

706
00:29:32.810 --> 00:29:34.230
Well, we're back from break.

707
00:29:34.410 --> 00:29:36.870
Back from our delightful conversation with Claire.

708
00:29:37.130 --> 00:29:37.290
Yes.

709
00:29:37.290 --> 00:29:39.090
Which is still lighting up my heart.

710
00:29:39.210 --> 00:29:39.570
Me too.

711
00:29:40.170 --> 00:29:42.190
And we're ready to break down this episode.

712
00:29:42.550 --> 00:29:44.350
Phyllis is going to arrive to work.

713
00:29:44.350 --> 00:29:46.430
It is raining cats and dogs.

714
00:29:46.590 --> 00:29:48.990
And I'd like to point out that she has a purple raincoat.

715
00:29:49.630 --> 00:29:50.030
Yes.

716
00:29:50.030 --> 00:29:52.530
The purple goes beyond her clothing, everyone.

717
00:29:52.910 --> 00:29:54.210
It's into her coat.

718
00:29:54.310 --> 00:29:55.870
Multiple purple accents.

719
00:29:56.170 --> 00:29:56.510
Mm-hmm.

720
00:29:57.150 --> 00:30:01.890
Jim is going to say, whenever it rains, Phyllis uses the same 12 cliché phrases.

721
00:30:02.150 --> 00:30:06.930
And he's promised to order hot chocolates for everyone if she says them all by noon.

722
00:30:07.310 --> 00:30:10.270
We got a fan question from Gabriella G.

723
00:30:10.370 --> 00:30:11.470
in Florida who said,

724
00:30:11.610 --> 00:30:14.990
What are all 12 of Phyllis's rainy day clichés?

725
00:30:15.170 --> 00:30:18.350
Jim holds them up on a list for a quick second, but it's hard to see.

726
00:30:18.350 --> 00:30:23.210
And I would love to be able to get all of the quotes right so I can continue to mention them every time it rains.

727
00:30:24.570 --> 00:30:27.950
Well, Gabriella, I froze on the screen.

728
00:30:28.150 --> 00:30:28.510
Mm-hmm.

729
00:30:28.830 --> 00:30:33.410
And along with the help of Dunderpedia, who also has the list, we can tell you.

730
00:30:33.830 --> 00:30:38.110
Here are Phyllis's 12 phrases every time it rains.

731
00:30:38.430 --> 00:30:40.470
One, the plants are going to love this.

732
00:30:41.090 --> 00:30:44.170
Two, I sure don't want to get stuck in this on the way home.

733
00:30:45.310 --> 00:30:47.710
Three, it's raining cats and dogs.

734
00:30:48.350 --> 00:30:51.090
Four, the humidity is going to make my hair freak.

735
00:30:51.870 --> 00:30:54.870
Five, the roads are slickest in the first half of the day.

736
00:30:55.770 --> 00:30:58.150
Six, I actually sleep better in the rain.

737
00:30:58.870 --> 00:31:01.550
Seven, we're paying for all the sun we got this summer.

738
00:31:02.030 --> 00:31:05.730
Eight, this weather just makes me want to stay home curled up with a good book.

739
00:31:06.410 --> 00:31:09.410
Nine, if we lived in Seattle, every day would be like this.

740
00:31:10.170 --> 00:31:14.190
Number 10, I wish we were allowed one rain check.

741
00:31:14.530 --> 00:31:17.310
Eleven, I love the smell after it rains.

742
00:31:17.650 --> 00:31:20.610
And 12, nobody knows how to drive in the rain.

743
00:31:20.910 --> 00:31:24.030
Those were Phyllis's 12 cliche statements.

744
00:31:24.370 --> 00:31:27.790
That's a lot of ground for her to cover to say before noon.

745
00:31:27.990 --> 00:31:28.210
Yeah.

746
00:31:28.210 --> 00:31:33.150
I was so tickled by this cold open, and I was talking to Josh about it, and he said,

747
00:31:33.270 --> 00:31:34.430
Ange, you or Phyllis?

748
00:31:34.750 --> 00:31:36.110
I was like, what are you talking about?

